Approximable mappings of two arguments as ternary neighbourhood relations, their elementwise action on pairs of ideal elements, and the correspondence with approximable maps out of a product.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`ApproximableMap₂`).** An approximable mapping of two arguments is a ternary relation on neighbourhoods that relates only neighbourhoods of the respective systems, relates the three masters, is closed under intersection in the third argument, and is monotone when the first two neighbourhoods shrink and the third expands.

**Definition (`toElementMap₂`).** The elementwise action of a binary approximable map on a pair of ideal elements collects those neighbourhoods related to some neighbourhoods of each argument.

*Proof.* Codomain membership, master, intersection, and upward closure follow from the corresponding axioms of the ternary relation. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_toElementMap₂`).** Membership in the elementwise image is existence of witnessing neighbourhoods of each argument related to the given neighbourhood.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`rel₂_iff_mem_principal`).** The ternary relation holds of neighbourhoods if and only if the corresponding neighbourhood belongs to the elementwise image of their principal filters.

*Proof.* One direction evaluates at the principals; the converse uses monotonicity to shrink to the given neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap₂_mono`).** The elementwise action is monotone in each argument for the approximation order.

*Proof.* Membership witnesses transport along the inequalities. ∎

**Theorem (`ApproximableMap₂.ext`).** Ternary relations that agree on all triples of neighbourhoods determine equal binary approximable maps.

*Proof.* Unpack the structure and compare the relation fields. ∎

**Definition (`ofMap₂`).** A binary approximable map determines an approximable map out of the product by relating a product neighbourhood to a codomain neighbourhood when the ternary relation holds of the two untagged factors.

*Proof.* Approximable-map axioms follow from the ternary axioms and the product neighbourhood laws. ∎

**Definition (`toMap₂`).** An approximable map out of a product determines a binary approximable map by evaluating at products of neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* The ternary axioms follow from the unary ones and nesting of product neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`ofMap₂_toMap₂`).** Converting a product map to a binary map and back recovers the original.

*Proof.* Compare relations using recovery of a product neighbourhood from its untaggings. ∎

**Theorem (`toMap₂_ofMap₂`).** Converting a binary map to a product map and back recovers the original.

*Proof.* Compare ternary relations using untagging of a product of neighbourhoods. ∎

**Definition (`map₂Equiv`).** Approximable maps out of a product are equivalent to binary approximable maps via the two conversions.

*Proof.* The round-trip identities. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap₂_toMap₂`).** The elementwise action of the binary form of a product map at a pair of ideal elements equals the unary elementwise action at their pairing.

*Proof.* Compare membership by unpacking product neighbourhoods into factors. ∎
